# Divisibility, the spine of number theory, modeled by the kernel. One number divides another when the second is a
# multiple of the first. The foundational law is that the multiples of a fixed number are closed under addition: if d
# divides both a and b, then d divides a + b. This is the distributive law d (j + k) = d j + d k, and it makes the
# multiples of d a subgroup of the integers, the fact behind ideals, congruences, and modular arithmetic. The kernel
# proves it by induction, on top of the commutative-monoid laws of addition it also proves here. Primes and greatest
# common divisors are built on this base.

form natural
  case zero
  case succ
    link prior, like natural

task plus
  take a, like natural
  take b, like natural
  like natural
  fork case, read a
    case zero
      send back
        read b
    case succ
      link ap
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            call plus
              read ap
              read b

task times
  take a, like natural
  take b, like natural
  like natural
  fork case, read a
    case zero
      send back
        make zero
    case succ
      link ap
      send back
        call plus
          read b
          call times
            read ap
            read b

# addition's monoid laws, needed to rearrange the sums in the distributive proof.
rule plus-zero-right
  seat a, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        read a
        make zero
      read a
  fold a

rule plus-succ-right
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        read a
        make succ
          bind prior
            read b
      make succ
        bind prior
          call plus
            read a
            read b
  fold a

rule plus-is-commutative
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        read a
        read b
      call plus
        read b
        read a
  fold a
    cite plus-zero-right
    cite plus-succ-right

rule plus-is-associative
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  seat c, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        call plus
          read a
          read b
        read c
      call plus
        read a
        call plus
          read b
          read c
  fold a

# definitional step exposing the multiplication recurrence, cited by the distributive proof.
rule times-succ-left-steps-out
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        make succ
          bind prior
            read a
        read b
      call plus
        read b
        call times
          read a
          read b
  calm hold

# THE DIVISOR-SUM LAW: a number dividing two others divides their sum. If a = d j and b = d k then a + b = d (j + k), the
# distributive law d (j + k) = d j + d k. Proved by induction on d; the successor case rearranges the four-term sum, which
# the associativity and commutativity of addition handle. So the multiples of d are closed under addition, a subgroup.
rule divisors-closed-under-sum
  seat d, like natural
  seat j, like natural
  seat k, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        read d
        call plus
          read j
          read k
      call plus
        call times
          read d
          read j
        call times
          read d
          read k
  fold d
    cite times-succ-left-steps-out
    cite plus-is-commutative
    cite plus-is-associative

# times distributes ALSO from the other side and zero is absorbing: d times 0 = 0, so every number divides zero (witness
# quotient zero). By induction on d.
rule times-zero-right
  seat d, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        read d
        make zero
      make zero
  fold d
